Let me think about the Handlebars layout system:

1. Register partials from `<templateDir>/partials/` directory
2. When rendering a page:
   a. Read the page's template (from frontmatter `template` field, or infer from path)
   b. Render the page's template with the page data (content = rendered HTML)
   c. If the page has a `layout` in frontmatter, wrap in layout:
      - Read the layout from `<templateDir>/layouts/<layout>.hbs`
      - Render the layout with `{{{body}}}` containing the page's rendered output

Wait, but in the layout, we might also want access to the page data. Let me think...

In the layout, `{{{body}}}` contains the inner template's rendered output. But the layout also has access to all the page data. So:

Page template (post.hbs):
```handlebars
<article>
  <h1>{{page.frontmatter.title}}</h1>
  {{{page.html}}}
</article>
```

Layout (base.hbs):
```handlebars
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>{{page.frontmatter.title}} - {{site.siteTitle}}</title>
</head>
<body>
  {{> header}}
  {{{body}}}
  {{> footer}}
</body>
</html>
```

The flow:
1. Render post.hbs with context `{ page, site, pages }` → produces inner HTML
2. Render base.hbs with same context + `body` = inner HTML

This pattern works well.

